1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:14,066
Until now we treated the parameters theta as one long vector. But network parameters live in matrices, one per layer.

2
00:00:14,066 --> 00:00:19,366
Recall from Xavier initialization: scaling problems come from the weight matrices.

3
00:00:19,366 --> 00:00:23,866
So let's measure the change ΔW in weight-matrix space instead.

4
00:00:23,866 --> 00:00:28,666
The matching inner product multiplies entries in the same position and adds them up.

5
00:00:28,666 --> 00:00:36,233
That number is the trace of G transpose times ΔW. Here both ways give five.

6
00:00:36,233 --> 00:00:42,366
Here is a 2 by 2 matrix A. Feed it every input of length one, the unit circle.

7
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:44,966
It maps the circle to an ellipse.

8
00:00:44,966 --> 00:00:52,200
The longest stretch is the largest singular value, 2.14. The shortest is 0.69.

9
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:58,300
The spectral norm of a matrix is exactly this: the largest amount it can stretch a unit vector.

10
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:06,933
So a spectral-norm ball of radius eta contains every matrix that stretches no input by more than eta.

11
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:14,600
Now the same question as before, for a matrix: the step of spectral size eta that lowers the linearized loss most.

12
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:22,366
Write the gradient's singular value decomposition: left vectors U, singular values sigma, right vectors V.

13
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:29,000
Trace is linear, so the inner product becomes a sum: each singular value times one small scalar.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:37,133
Each scalar is at most one, because B cannot stretch anything past one. So the total is at most the sum of singular values.

15
00:01:37,133 --> 00:01:45,800
And B equals U V transpose reaches that bound. So the best step is minus eta times U V transpose.

16
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:56,700
Take a random 5 by 3 gradient. Its singular values are 3.54, 2.15, 0.77, which sum to 6.46.

17
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:04,100
Normalized G gains only 4.21. Twenty thousand random steps never beat 4.80.

18
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:12,433
Only U V transpose reaches the full sum. The larger ball of allowed steps buys a larger predicted decrease.

19
00:02:12,433 --> 00:02:18,333
Look at what this step does to the gradient. The gradient has three very different singular values.

20
00:02:18,333 --> 00:02:25,466
U V transpose keeps the singular vectors, the directions, and replaces every singular value by one.

21
00:02:25,466 --> 00:02:32,400
Such a matrix is semi-orthogonal. Its condition number is one, versus 4.6 for the gradient.

22
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:39,466
Uniform step in all directions, and no domination by the largest singular values.

23
00:02:39,466 --> 00:02:46,400
This is the Shampoo optimizer of Gupta and coauthors, 2017 and 2018.

24
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:53,100
Shampoo scales the gradient on both sides by inverse fourth roots. That also gives U V transpose.

25
00:02:53,100 --> 00:03:01,500
A variant of Shampoo, by Dahl and coauthors in 2023, won AlgoPerf, a competition for training algorithms.

26
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:10,200
Two questions remain: how to avoid computing the SVD every step, and how to set the step size for layers of different shapes.

